<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto of Bamberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1060–1139</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Apostle of Pomerania, Saint Otto of Bamberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> (c. 1060–1139) was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Bamberg from 1102 and a missionary to the Pomeranians. On two journeys, in 1124–1125 and 1128, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartislaw-i-duke-of-pomerania">Wartislaw I</a>, he converted the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, baptising thousands at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrzyce">Pyrzyce</a> and other towns. He was canonised in 1189 and is honoured as the Apostle of Pomerania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-of-bamberg.jpg" alt="Otto of Bamberg" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pr%C3%BCfening_Klosterkirche_-_Romanische_Fresken_3b_Otto_von_Bamberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
